Assistant: The error persists, and it seems to be related to module loading. Let's try a different approach by adding error boundaries and handling the dynamic import differently. We'll also mock some of the providers to reduce dependencies:

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/ServerPage-2e79329a.tsx">
<change>
<description>Simplify providers and add error handling for dynamic imports</description>
<content>
import React, { Suspense } from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import dynamic from 'next/dynamic';

// Use dynamic import with no SSR to avoid server-side issues
const ImportedComponent = dynamic(
  () => import('../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/settings/platform/new/page').catch(() => {
    return () => <div>Failed to load component</div>;
  }),
  { 
    ssr: false,
    loading: () => <div>Loading...</div>
  }
);

// Mock providers to reduce external dependencies
const MockProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => <>{children}</>;
const MockTRPCProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => <>{children}</>;
const MockFeatureProvider = ({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) => <>{children}</>;

// Simple error boundary component
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component<
  { children: React.ReactNode },
  { hasError: boolean }
> {
  constructor(props: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError() {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return <div>Something went wrong.</div>;
    }
    return this.props.children;
  }
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    params: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ id: "123" }),
      label: "Params",
    },
    searchParams: {
      type: "string",
      value: JSON.stringify({ query: "test" }),
      label: "Search Params",
    },
  });

  const props = {
    params: JSON.parse(state.params.value),
    searchParams: JSON.parse(state.searchParams.value),
  };

  return (
    <ErrorBoundary>
      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
        <MockProvider>
          <MockProvider>
            <MockProvider>
              <MockTRPCProvider>
                <MockProvider>
                  <MockFeatureProvider>
                    <div style={{ padding: '20px' }}>
                      <ImportedComponent {...props} />
                    </div>
                  </MockFeatureProvider>
                </MockProvider>
              </MockTRPCProvider>
            </MockProvider>
          </MockProvider>
        </MockProvider>
      </Suspense>
    </ErrorBoundary>
  );
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

Key changes made:

1. Replaced direct import with `dynamic` import from Next.js
2. Added error handling for the dynamic import
3. Disabled SSR for the component
4. Created simple mock providers instead of using real ones to reduce dependencies
5. Added a custom ErrorBoundary component
6. Simplified the provider structure using mock providers
7. Added basic styling wrapper
8. Added proper error catching for the dynamic import

This approach should be more resilient because:
1. It handles dynamic import failures gracefully
2. It reduces external dependencies that might cause issues
3. It provides proper error boundaries and fallbacks
4. It uses Next.js's dynamic import which is better suited for this environment

The error message suggests there might be an issue with the module resolution, so using Next.js's dynamic import with error handling should help resolve the issue. If the component still fails to load, at least we'll have proper error handling and fallback UI in place.

